    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Guide</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Happens If It Gets Windy During a Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>10 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Usually the skipper slows down, moves everyone towards the back and, if the breeze keeps building, turns for home earlier than planned. On Madeira&#x27;s south coast a rising wind makes the ride wetter and bouncier long before it becomes dangerous, and the skipper acts at that first stage.</p>

      <h2>Does the wind really pick up in the afternoon?</h2><p>Often, but not as a rule. On sunny days the land warms through the morning and a sea breeze tends to build, so the hours after lunch are frequently a little livelier than the first hours of the day. Some mornings are breezy and some afternoons are glassy. The forecast for your date beats any rule of thumb.</p><p>What changes during a trip is usually small: a ruffled surface instead of a smooth one, a few white crests offshore, a bit more spray at the bow. That is a normal day at sea, not an emergency. The question is what happens if it keeps building, and the answer is a short sequence of decisions the skipper makes early.</p><h2>What does the skipper do when the wind rises?</h2><p>A skipper has three tools: speed, route and time. They are used roughly in this order, each one a small step, long before anything feels dramatic.</p><ol><li><strong>Slow down.</strong> Speed is what turns small waves into hard knocks and spray. Dropping a few knots makes the ride softer and drier at once.</li><li><strong>Change the angle.</strong> Taking waves slightly off the bow, rather than straight on or straight from the side, smooths the motion.</li><li><strong>Move people.</strong> Anyone sitting at the front is asked to come back towards the middle and the stern, where the boat moves least.</li><li><strong>Rethink the stop.</strong> A swim stop that was perfect at 11:00 can turn choppy by 15:00. The skipper may pick the more sheltered of the two, or skip swimming if the water by the rocks is restless.</li><li><strong>Turn earlier.</strong> A trip planned to reach Ribeira Brava might turn under Cabo Girão instead, which keeps the ride home short.</li><li><strong>Head back.</strong> If the trend is clearly worse, the boat returns to Funchal. That decision is the skipper's, and a good one explains it.</li></ol><p>On Chifbay trips the same logic applies before departure. If the sea is not safe for your slot, the trip is called off, and you decide between moving it and taking a complete refund. A day that is merely breezy may still run, with the plan adjusted along the way. If you want to know how a shortened trip would be handled on your booking, ask us on WhatsApp before you pay.</p><h2>How windy is too windy for a small boat?</h2><p>There is no single number, because swell, wind direction and the shape of the coast all count. The Beaufort scale still helps you read a forecast. The average speeds below are the Met Office figures for each force.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Force</th><th>Name and average speed</th><th>What it usually means on a small boat trip</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3</td><td>Gentle breeze, about 9 knots</td><td>Pleasant. A ruffled sea, a cooling breeze, dry seats</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Moderate breeze, about 13 knots</td><td>Lively at speed, some spray at the front, still comfortable further back</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Fresh breeze, about 19 knots</td><td>Bumpy and wet when running into it. Expect a slower pace and changes to the plan</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Strong breeze, about 24 knots</td><td>Rough water. Not a day for a pleasure trip along the cliffs</td></tr></tbody></table><p>One more number is worth knowing. Recreational boats sold in the EU carry a design category, and category C means a boat designed for winds up to force 6 and waves up to about 2 metres. That is the limit the hull was built to survive, not a target for a holiday morning. Careful skippers stay well inside it.</p><p>Wind direction matters as much as strength. A breeze blowing off the land onto the sea leaves the water close to the cliffs fairly flat, while the same strength blowing from the west or south-west piles waves onto this coast. The numbers worth watching are explained in <a href="/posts/how-to-read-the-sea-forecast-madeira-boat-trip">how to read the sea forecast before a boat trip</a>.</p><h2>Will you get wet if the wind picks up?</h2><p>A little, mostly if you sit at the front. Spray comes off the bow when it meets a wave and the wind carries it back over the deck. The canopy and the rear seats stay much drier.</p><ul><li><strong>Sit at the back or in the middle</strong> on the way home, which is usually the fastest part.</li><li><strong>Bring a thin windproof layer,</strong> even in August. Wet skin in a breeze cools fast.</li><li><strong>Keep your phone in a pocket or a pouch</strong> as soon as the white crests appear.</li><li><strong>Tie or remove your hat.</strong> Wind takes loose hats straight into the Atlantic.</li></ul><h2>Why is a windy afternoon rarely dangerous on this coast?</h2><p>Because everything about the trip works in your favour. The route is coastal and short: from Funchal, Cabo Girão is about 5 nautical miles away and Fajã dos Padres a little more. The mountains shelter this side of the island from the usual north-east wind. Câmara de Lobos harbour sits along the way. The trips last two to three hours, so the forecast for that window is reliable. And the person deciding knows this water and has no reason to push on.</p><p>Wind becomes a real problem when people ignore it: staying out for one more photo, running fast into steep waves, swimming by rocks while the swell grows. A good skipper avoids all three, and that is why the usual result of a windy afternoon is a slower ride home and a few salty sunglasses.</p><h2>When should you choose another day?</h2><p>If the forecast shows a strong breeze for your slot and you get seasick easily, change it. Take the 10:00 <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> instead of the afternoon one, or move the date. Cancelling a Chifbay booking is free until 24 hours before departure. The same thinking applies to the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a>: a calm evening with soft light beats a breezy one where everyone sits huddled behind the canopy. For how cancellations work when the sea itself is the problem, see <a href="/posts/what-happens-to-a-boat-trip-in-madeira-in-bad-weather">what happens to a boat trip in bad weather</a>.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can you still swim if it gets windy?</h3><p>Sometimes. If the water at the stop stays calm, yes. If waves start to break against the rocks, the skipper may move to a more sheltered spot or skip the swim.</p><h3>Is the morning trip less windy than the afternoon one?</h3><p>Often a little, because sea breezes tend to build as the land warms. It is a tendency, not a promise, so check the forecast for your own date.</p><h3>What should I wear if wind is forecast?</h3><p>A thin windproof jacket over your clothes, shoes with grip, and a hat that ties on. Keep a dry layer in your bag for the ride home.</p><h3>Who decides whether to turn back?</h3><p>The skipper. They watch the sea through the whole trip and change speed, route or timing as the wind changes.</p>

      <blockquote>A breeze changes the plan by a few knots and a few minutes. That is the whole drama, when someone sensible is steering.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

